Equivalence Groups Found
└── Modbus TCP client (read/write holding registers to industrial PLC/RTU device)
    ├── Candidates : pymodbus, modbus-tk, umodbus
    ├── Used APIs  : ModbusTcpClient, client.connect, client.read_holding_registers, client.write_register, rr.isError, rr.registers, wr.isError
    ├── Pitfalls   : pymodbus's isError()/registers response object API differs significantly from modbus-tk's tuple-based register results and umodbus's 
    │   function-based API; The 'unit' keyword argument (slave/unit id) is named/passed differently across libraries (e.g., slave_id in modbus-tk); Connection 
    │   lifecycle (connect/close) and exception handling for timeouts/CRC errors differ across libraries; modbus-tk and umodbus have different 
    │   synchronous/threading models and client instantiation patterns than pymodbus's ModbusTcpClient
    ├── Constraints: requires_connector
    └── The file uses pymodbus's ModbusTcpClient for reading/writing Modbus TCP holding registers, dependency-injected into ModbusIO. modbus-tk and umodbus 
        both support Modbus TCP master/client functionality with comparable read/write-register operations and could functionally replace pymodbus for the same
        purpose (talking to a Modbus TCP slave device). minimalmodbus is excluded from this group despite being suggested, because it is designed primarily for
        serial (RTU/ASCII) communication and does not natively provide a TCP client (ModbusTcpClient) equivalent — it would not be a direct substitute for this
        file's actual TCP usage without an additional serial-to-TCP gateway, which is a different architecture, not a like-for-like swap.

